meta {
  name: An unknown requisition is data, not an error
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/mcp
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  mcp-session-id: {{mcpSessionId}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "jsonrpc": "2.0",
    "id": 20,
    "method": "tools/call",
    "params": {
      "name": "get_requisition",
      "arguments": { "job_id": "{{missingJobId}}" }
    }
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
}

tests {
  test("HTTP 200 and isError false — this is a successful call", function() {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
    expect(res.getBody().result.isError).to.be.false;
  });

  test("the miss is reported inside the payload", function() {
    expect(res.getBody().result.structuredContent).to.eql({ error: "no requisition J9999" });
  });
}

docs {
  The REST project answers this with `404`. Here it is `200`, `isError: false`, and an
  `{"error": ...}` object in the payload — and that is the tool working exactly as written:

      return job or {"error": f"no requisition {job_id}"}

  **An MCP tool's return value is the next thing the model reads.** A returned `{"error": …}`
  is something it can act on; an unhandled exception is just a failed call it will retry.

  The message could still be better. *"unknown requisition J9999; call `shortlist` with an id
  from `get_requisition`"* tells the model what to do next — without that, a weak model tries
  J9998, then J9997, until something stops it. Compare with *Calling a tool that does not
  exist*, where the framework raises the error rather than the tool returning it.
}
